  "body": "The maintainer asked for this work on 2026-11-15:\n\n> All tests should be broken up like the heavy lane into small \"single\" runs. That showed in the last cut gate on github actions to be alot more efficient than running large batches of tests together..\n\nWhat happens now:\n- The heavy lanes of the release tier run one step for each test binary (#1714, #1783) and the longest tests are split (#1715).\n- The last cut gate (run 43739171721, `cut.yml` stage gate) had 187 steps and a wall time of 66.4 minutes. One step failed, and its name gave the binary and the test immediately.\n- The debug suites of the quick, batch and release tiers still run as large groups: one `cargo test` call for many binaries of a crate.\n\nWhat is wanted:\n- `cargo xtask gate` plans one step for each test binary in each tier, not only in the heavy lanes.\n- Each step has its own log, its own time and its own pass or fail line.\n- `--only` and `--failed` can run one such step again.\n- The `#[test]` count before and after is the same. No test is lost.\n\nOpen points for the worker:\n- Measure the wall time of the batch tier before and after, on the post-merge runner and on Windows. Write the two readings in the PR.\n- Keep one build for each crate, then run the binaries from the cargo JSON artifacts. Do not build one time for each step.\n- Decide how many steps run in parallel (AGENTS.md permits `-j 6` and four gate lanes at most).\n- `xtask/` and the `gate` skill are process files. The PR body has the line `Process change: yes`.\n\nRelated: #919 (gate cost umbrella), #900 (compile cache).\n\nDone means: the quick, batch and release tiers plan one step for each test binary, the test count is the same, and the PR gives the measured wall times before and after.\n",
